Speed up Documentation/install-webdoc.sh on slow-fork platforms.

Documentation/install-webdoc.sh copies ~1100 HTML/AsciiDoc files
into the doc-html package staging directory. The original loop
forks four times per file (`rm`, command-substituted `dirname`,
`mkdir -p`, `cp`), plus a per-file `expr` in the prune loop, plus
an `echo | sed` to precompute the prefix-stripping pattern.

On MSYS2 / Windows ARM64 each fork+exec costs roughly 3x what it
does on x86_64, so this single script dominates the
package_*-doc-html build step (observed at ~5.5 minutes on ARM64
vs ~1.8 minutes on x64; collapsing the fork pattern brings it down
into the same ballpark).

Three changes, all of which preserve the exact output tree the
loop currently produces for the doc-html packaging case:

  - Hoist the per-file `mkdir -p $(dirname "$T/$h")` out of the
    loop. The globbed source paths only ever live in four
    directories (`$T`, `$T/howto`, `$T/technical`, `$T/RelNotes`),
    so create them once up front.

  - Drop the per-file `rm -f "$T/$h"`. `cp` overwrites regular
    files. The only behavioural difference would be when the
    destination is a symlink; the only symlink this script ever
    creates is `$T/index.html` (last line), which is not produced
    by any source glob in the copy loop.

  - Replace `expr "$th" : "$strip_leading"'\(.*\)'` in the prune
    loop with POSIX `${th#"$T"/}` parameter expansion. This also
    removes the `strip_leading=$(echo "$T/" | sed 's|.|.|g')`
    precomputation and incidentally fixes a latent issue where
    `$T` containing regex metacharacters would be quietly papered
    over by replacing every character with `.`.

Net result for a fresh doc-html package build (1127 source files):
~4500 process spawns reduced to ~1130, with no observable change
in the resulting tree.

diff --git a/Documentation/install-webdoc.sh b/Documentation/install-webdoc.sh
--- a/Documentation/install-webdoc.sh
+++ b/Documentation/install-webdoc.sh
@@ -2,6 +2,14 @@
 
 T="$1"
 
+# Pre-create the destination subdirectories once, instead of running
+# `mkdir -p $(dirname "$T/$h")` (subshell + dirname fork + mkdir
+# fork) inside the per-file loop below. The fork-per-file pattern is
+# the dominant cost when this script runs under MSYS2 on Windows
+# ARM64, where fork+exec is roughly 3x slower than on x86_64; the
+# globbed paths only ever resolve into these four directories.
+mkdir -p "$T" "$T/howto" "$T/technical" "$T/RelNotes"
+
 for h in \
 	*.adoc *.html \
 	howto/*.adoc howto/*.html \
@@ -17,18 +25,19 @@ do
 		:; # up to date
 	else
 		echo >&2 "# install $h $T/$h"
-		rm -f "$T/$h"
-		mkdir -p $(dirname "$T/$h")
 		cp "$h" "$T/$h"
 	fi
 done
-strip_leading=$(echo "$T/" | sed -e 's|.|.|g')
 for th in \
 	"$T"/*.html "$T"/*.adoc \
 	"$T"/howto/*.adoc "$T"/howto/*.html \
 	"$T"/technical/*.adoc "$T"/technical/*.html
 do
-	h=$(expr "$th" : "$strip_leading"'\(.*\)')
+	# Use POSIX parameter expansion instead of `expr "$th" : ...`
+	# (one fork per existing destination file) to strip the "$T/"
+	# prefix. This also removes the need for the `strip_leading`
+	# pre-computation and the `echo | sed` it used to set up.
+	h=${th#"$T"/}
 	case "$h" in
 	RelNotes-*.adoc | index.html) continue ;;
 	esac
